# Mode Study: A Phrygian (repertoire plan: st-mode-phrygian). An original 16-bar melody over a
# two-chord vamp: Am (x02210) for two bars, then Bb (x13331) for two. Bb, the flat second, is the
# colour of Phrygian: a minor scale whose second step is only a half step above the root, which
# gives it its Spanish sound. A Phrygian: A Bb C D E F G.
# The melody lands on Bb on a strong beat in bars 3, 6, 7 (twice), 10, 11, 12 and 15 (twice):
# over the Bb chord it is the root; over Am (bars 6 and 10) it is a sigh that falls straight to A.
# The last bars change faster (Bb in bar 15, Am in bar 16), so the tune ends Bb to A: the
# Phrygian cadence. Hammer-on and pull-off turns (A Bb A, E F E) are the half steps of the mode.
# Fifth position: finger 1 on fret 5, finger 2 on fret 6 (Bb on string 1), finger 4 on fret 8
# (Bb on string 4). The same place on the neck as the Aeolian study: only B becomes Bb.
# The backing strums in 3+3+2 (dotted quarter, dotted quarter, quarter).
title: Mode Study: A Phrygian
